READINESS AND LIVENESS TRUTH (R23) — audit host, 2026-09-03T21:15:02Z
HEAD 04e380f · every command below was run on the audit host; the output is real.

── Liveness is up; readiness is degraded, and says which subsystem ────
$ curl -sS -o /dev/null -w %{http_code} http://127.0.0.1:3002/api/health
200

$ curl -sSi http://127.0.0.1:3002/api/health/ready | head -1
HTTP/1.1 503 Service Unavailable
$ curl -sS http://127.0.0.1:3002/api/health/ready
{
    "ok": false,
    "status": "degraded",
    "dataDir": true,
    "toolchain": false,
    "database": true,
    "checks": [
        {
            "name": "database",
            "healthy": true
        }
    ]
}

── Why: one of the seven binaries in binaryInfo is genuinely absent ───
$ command -v soffice    → not found
$ command -v gs         → /opt/homebrew/bin/gs
$ command -v qpdf       → /opt/homebrew/bin/qpdf
$ command -v pdftoppm   → /opt/homebrew/bin/pdftoppm
$ command -v pdfinfo    → /opt/homebrew/bin/pdfinfo
$ command -v tesseract  → /opt/homebrew/bin/tesseract
$ command -v ocrmypdf   → /opt/homebrew/bin/ocrmypdf

── What this is and is not ────────────────────────────────────────────
PASS, and the strongest form of it: readiness is red on a host where a
required binary really is missing, while liveness stays green. That is the
split the two endpoints exist to make — liveness answers "is the process
alive", readiness answers "should traffic come here" — and it is what
mutation O (readiness green while a dependency is unavailable) tried to break.

The missing binary is ENVIRONMENTAL: LibreOffice is not installed on this
macOS audit host. The shipped image installs it (Dockerfile apt line), and as
of 04e380f a test walks binaryInfo and fails if the image stops installing any
of the seven.

Two operational consequences, for the operator rather than the code:
  · A load balancer reading /api/health/ready would never route to THIS host.
    That is correct behaviour, not a defect.
  · The container HEALTHCHECK asks /api/health, which does not look at the
    toolchain. A container missing a binary is therefore "healthy" and not
    ready — deliberate (a restart would not install a binary), but it means
    the deploy signal to watch is readiness, not the container's health state.

The readiness dependency probe caches for 30 s (DEP_CACHE_MS). A dependency
fixed seconds ago still reads false until the cache expires; a rollback
verification must wait that out rather than conclude the fix failed.
